    {
      "id": "q5",
      "questionType": "text",
      "question": "Describe how density-dependent and density-independent factors create different feedback loops in population dynamics.",
      "maxScore": 7,
      "timeLimit": 200,
      "correctAnswer": "Density-dependent factors (like competition, predation, disease) create negative feedback loops where their impact increases with population density, helping regulate population size. Density-independent factors (like weather, natural disasters) create external disturbances that can affect populations regardless of size, potentially disrupting existing feedback loops. The interaction between these factors creates complex population dynamics where multiple feedback loops operate simultaneously.",
